Link to game: https://jazzswush.itch.io/project-1-dodge-for-your-life

For this project I wanted to create something simple and like my Creative Coding 2 class 
assignment. Though I had to review Unity since I went on summer break and never had a 
chance to work on any Unity projects. So, I was rusty again and had to relearn, but once I 
started playing around with playground in Unity, it slowly came back to me on things that I 
could remember more. I did not use any if/statements since this game was very simple. I 
made a space game where you dodge asteroids and aliens. The player must collect 5 total 
stars to win the game before they lose all their health. I also added some hearts to help 
achieve their goal. I kind of made it hard to make it challenging to test the coordination of 
player movement. I found that there were a lot of inconsistencies when it comes to player 
movements even after changing different values in speed, friction or mass. So, hopefully in 
itch.io it is not too fast or slow for player movements. I find working in space a bit hard 
when you add the playground UI system because it kind of takes part of the screen and not 
sure if I moved it, that it would ruin the screen placement in the game. I did find some 
limitations to playgrounds like bounding barriers, well at least I could not find it in the 
inspector area. I tried a different method, but the asteroids and aliens became a limitation too. 
So, I just scratched that idea, and hope players just stay in the camera view as their main 
objective is on screen. Another limitation would add a timer. I initially wanted it to be timed 
and see how long the player could last, but I could not figure out how to add a timer that I could remember. 
I instead made it a point system. This also made me rework the game on the difficulty level. 
I found tweaking the speed on Unity and playing it on itch.io is completely different as well. 
I had to keep reuploading, so it was good enough speed. Is there a way to work around this please let me know. 
Overall, I enjoyed going back and relearning how to use Unity with the help of playground assets. 
